                                  LICENSE

This tree contains two layers of authorship.

  * Thomas -- a compiler and runtime for a language compatible with the one
    described in "Dylan(TM) an object-oriented dynamic language" (Apple
    Computer Eastern Research and Technology, April 1992) -- written in
    Scheme at Digital Equipment Corporation's Cambridge Research Laboratory
    by Matt Birkholz, Jim Miller and Ron Weiss, and released in 1992 under
    the notice reproduced in full below.

  * src/ -- a translation of Thomas from Scheme into Common Lisp.
    (Thomas's own Scheme sources, which lived in a directory of the same
    name, are not part of this tree.)

    Copyright 2026 Blake McBride.

    The translation is a derivative work of Thomas.  The Digital Equipment
    Corporation terms below govern the use, modification and distribution
    of the whole of it, and this copyright notice claims authorship of the
    translation only; it grants no rights beyond those Digital granted.

Also in this tree, and covered by the same notice: dylan-examples.dyl,
examples-from-book.text, README.thomas, RELEASE, and the three
transcripts in src/reference/.

One directory is another's work under another license: runtime/mps/ is
the Memory Pool System, a garbage collector by Ravenbrook Limited, under
the BSD 2-clause license in runtime/mps/license.txt.  See
runtime/mps/README.md.

                            WHAT THIS MEANS

The following is an informational summary.  It does not modify, qualify or
add to the terms below, which are the operative ones.

Digital's notice permits use, copying and modification, and permits
distribution to third parties who agree to abide by the same notice, but
only where the software is "not offered for sale or transferred for
compensation".  That restriction on selling means the notice does not meet
the Open Source Definition or the Free Software Definition, and it is not
compatible with the GPL or with permissive licenses such as MIT, BSD or
Apache.  The software is source-available and freely redistributable, not
open source.

The notice requires that both the copyright notice and the permission
notice appear in all copies, derivative works and modified versions, and
any portions thereof.  They appear at the head of every source file in
this tree as well as in this file; keep them there.

Placing any part of this tree under a different license would require
permission from the present owner of Digital's copyright, by way of the
Compaq (1998) and Hewlett-Packard (2002) acquisitions and the 2015
Hewlett-Packard split.

Trademark, separately from copyright: the 1992 authors named the system
Thomas rather than Dylan on purpose, writing in README.thomas that "Thomas
is NOT
Dylan(TM).  We have not received approval for the use of the trademark."
The Common Lisp translation in src/ names its package and its entry points
Dylan.  Nothing in the notice below bears on trademark either way.

